title start
kind: research
tags: business-plan research retention-and-watch-time-evidence
How do retention and replays affect the distribution of short videos
title end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end
paragraph start
Question: how do retention and replays affect distribution. Method: industry articles. Result: completion and rewatches are the strongest signals on TikTok, YouTube looks at the shape of the retention curve, and looping raises watch time per view. Limit: the thresholds quoted by blogs are unverified.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end
paragraph start
Retention is the signal the brand controls most through editing, so this page records what is known.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end
paragraph start
Read industry articles and a watch time glossary.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Results
title end
paragraph start
TikTok accelerates distribution when people watch a video more than once without scrolling away.
paragraph end
paragraph start
One source says the completion rate needed for broad distribution rose from about fifty to about seventy percent between 2024 and 2026. This is unverified.
paragraph end
paragraph start
A source on Reels says viewers must pass the first three seconds and aims for about sixty percent retention. This is unverified.
paragraph end
paragraph start
YouTube looks at the shape of the retention curve, not only total minutes, and a video that loops raises watch time per view.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end
paragraph start
Shorter videos finish more often, and the first seconds decide most of the result. Measure the own retention before trusting any number from a blog.
paragraph end
paragraph start
todo: measure own retention on the first twenty posts
Compare with the quoted numbers.
paragraph end
